  <data-orientation>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{C8A41BCC-617F-42EA-8DE6-5C790F1A82D0}' />
      <desc>This visualization examines each department&apos;s share of the overall student population. The calculated percentage places the departments in the context of the entire dataset rather than comparing their student counts alone. From the results, it is clear that the Physics department holds the largest share of the student population at 25.5%, while Biology holds the smallest at 13.5%. Between these figures, Computer Science and Chemistry each account for 23% of the population, while Mathematics represents approximately 15%.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{F9A51566-66D5-4B5E-8256-7755860E49E9}' />
      <desc>This visualization examines the age composition of students within each graduation cohort. The stacked bars show how different age groups contribute to each graduation year, providing a more detailed view of cohort composition than comparing average ages alone. Judging by the results, it appears that the year 2030 has the largest cohort, with 15 students graduating between the ages of 18–20, 12 students between 21–23, and 10 students ranging from 24–25. In contrast, the smallest cohort appears in 2026, where 7 students between the ages of 18–20 are graduating, 10 are between 21–23, and 6 are between 24–25.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{D4CAF07C-B985-4F1F-85E7-8BBFC62A741B}' />
      <desc>This heat map compares the composition of GPA performance levels within each department. By grouping individual GPA values into performance categories, the visualization shows where students are concentrated within each department. Physics has the highest number of students in the 3.50+ range, with 17 students, along with 15 in the 3.00–3.49 range, showing a strong concentration in the higher GPA ranges. In comparison, Biology has the fewest students in the 3.00–3.49 range, with 4, while Mathematics has the fewest in the Below 2.50 category, also with 4. Computer Science has 15 students in the 3.50+ range, while Chemistry appears to have the most evenly distributed representation across the GPA categories.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{E546D12C-E9DB-4AFE-A454-35C51223FEDB}' />
      <desc>This heat map analysis examines how student age groups are represented within each academic department. The varying cell intensity highlights differences in the concentration of students across age groups and departments. The results show that Physics has the highest concentration of students in the 21–23 age group, with 22 students, followed by Chemistry with 20 students in the same range. Computer Science has the largest representation of students aged 18–20, with approximately 19 students, while Biology has the smallest representation of students aged 24–25, with 5 students, showing a stronger concentration among the younger age groups.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{BC9F1B17-0F0D-4E59-B0FA-CC33276674D8}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the proportion of students achieving a GPA of 3.50 or higher within each department. Unlike a simple student count, the percentage accounts for differences in department size and provides a more comparable measure of higher academic achievement. When looking closer at the results, 33.3% of students in the Physics department have a GPA of 3.50 or higher, with Computer Science following behind at 32.6%. In comparison, only 18.5% of students in Biology have a GPA of 3.50 or higher, while Chemistry has the second-lowest percentage at 19.6%. Mathematics falls between Computer Science and Physics, with 23.3% of its student population having a GPA of 3.50 or higher.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{11214E2E-3FFD-45A4-9C43-5AD7B3A177F3}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the average age of students across academic departments to examine whether the typical age of students varies among the different areas of study. Using the average age provides a department-level comparison that builds upon the broader age distributions examined earlier. The results show differences in average student age across the departments, with Physics having the highest average age at approximately 21.73 years and Biology having the lowest at 21.19 years. The differences indicate that the typical age of students varies to some degree across the academic areas represented in the dataset.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{FFC3F6C8-72A5-48EA-9BF4-8C70C32C1366}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the range of GPA values within each academic department by measuring the difference between the highest and lowest recorded GPAs. The comparison provides another perspective on academic variation by showing which departments have the widest and narrowest ranges of student performance. The results show that the Mathematics Department has the widest range between its highest and lowest GPAs, with a range of 1.97, while Biology has the narrowest range, with a GPA difference of exactly 1.88.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
  </data-orientation>
